LibonOS
=======

**A Linux-based operating system.** LibonOS is built on the Linux kernel and ships with its own desktop shell, application store, and package manager — so it boots into a complete, usable system rather than a bare kernel.

The kernel in this tree is the Linux kernel; everything that makes it *LibonOS* is the **LibonUI** stack described below.

## What LibonOS is

- A Linux-based OS — the kernel is Linux, the user experience is LibonUI.
- A desktop that works on first boot: setup, login, session, and a populated desktop.
- A package and update story that is deliberately simple and hard to get wrong.
- One design language shared by the shell, the installer, the terminal and the command line, because the design tokens are data.

## LibonUI — the desktop shell

LibonUI lives in `libonui/`, in three Rust crates:

| crate | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `libonui-core` | Everything with a rule in it: design tokens, versions and the update policy, accounts, the window manager, the package backends. No UI toolkit. |
| `libonui-app` | The shell itself — `egui`/`eframe`. Setup, login, desktop, Store, Settings, terminal, file manager, text editor. |
| `libonui-cli` | `libon`, the command-line twin of the Store and the Updates pane. |

```text
cargo run -p libonui-app            # setup on a fresh machine, otherwise login
cargo run -p libonui-app -- --demo  # straight into a populated desktop
cargo run -p libonui-cli -- status  # libon
cargo test --workspace
```

## First boot

Setup asks for four things, one screen at a time: your **first name and password**, the **package manager** to use, and whether to send telemetry. The name and password become the local account; the password is stored as a salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 digest and never in clear. The login screen afterwards checks against that digest.

The account, the settings and the session live under the user's state directory, so `--state <dir>` gives you a throwaway machine to try things in.

## The package manager

Three backends, one interface (`PackageManager` in `libonui-core`):

- **libon** — preinstalled and the default. `libon install <app>` resolves the name to a publisher's AppImage, downloads it to `~/Applications`, and writes a `.desktop` entry. `libon update` updates in place.
- **APT** — Debian and Ubuntu repositories.
- **pacman** — Arch.

The choice is recorded in Settings and honoured by both the Store and the CLI. A backend that is not installed on the machine refuses the install and says so rather than appearing to work: libon is always present, APT and pacman may not be.

## Updates, and why there are two kinds

LibonOS separates two operations that other systems blur together:

- **In-place update** (`1.0.0` -> `1.0.1`, `1.1.0`) — patches and minor releases. Available from Settings *and* from the terminal with `libon update`.
- **Full system upgrade** (`1.0.0` -> `2.0.0`) — rewrites the system image. It is offered **only from Settings**, behind a confirmation that says what it will do. `libon upgrade` explains the rule instead of running it.

That asymmetry is the point: a stray command should never be able to rebuild the machine underneath the user. The policy itself lives in `libonui-core::version`, so the UI and the CLI cannot drift apart.

## What a good OS ships with

`libonui-core::system` is the single source of truth: 16 preinstalled apps — Firefox first and pinned to the dock, then Files, Terminal, Store, Settings, and the rest — plus a 14-point capability checklist (browser, file management, terminal, Store, updates, network, audio, Bluetooth, printing, power, accessibility, security, backup, security updates) that Settings reports on.

## Screenshots

`libonui --shots <dir>` renders every screen to PNG without a display server and writes an `index.html` contact sheet. The images in `libonui/artifacts/` are produced that way, so they are the real rendered output rather than a mock-up:

```text
xvfb-run -a -s "-screen 0 1440x900x24" cargo run -p libonui-app -- --shots artifacts
```

## Design

A deep violet-black canvas with one violet-to-cyan accent pair. Colour carries meaning — action, focus, status — never decoration. The tokens are data (`libonui-core::tokens`), so the shell, the installer, the terminal and the CLI all read the same values, and the contrast ratios are testable.

## Repository layout

| path | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `libonui/` | The LibonUI desktop shell, Store and `libon` manager (Rust). |
| `Documentation/` | Linux kernel documentation. |
| kernel sources | The Linux kernel this OS is built on. |

The kernel documentation under `Documentation/` is the Linux kernel's own and still applies to the kernel itself.

